    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Understood. I'm also concerned about the small, persistent disruptions—not necessarily major breaches, but those small, irritating issues that slowly erode profit margins or delay production, like slight variations in material quality or minor delays in shipping. How do we handle those in the contract so they don't just become an accepted part of doing business with them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Address these minor issues via Service Level Agreements (SLAs) that define acceptable performance thresholds for quality and delivery punctuality. Instead of waiting for a major breach, include tiered penalty structures, such as liquidated damages, for falling short of these specified SLAs. Furthermore, incorporate a mandatory \"corrective action plan\" requirement, forcing the supplier to address small deviations quickly rather than having them accepted as business-as-usual. Regularly scheduled performance reviews linked to these metrics will hold them accountable throughout the contract life.",
      "source": "target",
